WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:03.380
In Search of Dharma.

2
00:00:04.932 --> 00:00:06.800
8: Creating Dharmas.

3
00:00:08.767 --> 00:00:13.040
We have always created our dharmas and
told ourselves we found them.

4
00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.600
What becomes possible, and what becomes
dangerous,

5
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.000
once we admit it and start building on
purpose?

6
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.380
A woman sits on a cushion in a small flat
in a large city.

7
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.240
A handbook of Stoic sayings lies on the
floor beside her,

8
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.380
a meditation app glows on her phone,

9
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:34.140
and she is trying to assemble, from those
scraps and a few others,

10
00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:36.100
something she can actually live by.

11
00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.140
We met her in Part 7.

12
00:00:38.475 --> 00:00:42.900
She is doing the oldest human work there
is, alone, mostly without help.

13
00:00:44.666 --> 00:00:47.380
Elsewhere, a team at an AI company sits
down

14
00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.900
to write what it openly calls a
constitution.

15
00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:54.400
Not a constitution for a country: a list
of values for a machine,

16
00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:57.560
governing how it answers when a stranger
asks it how to live.

17
00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:02.360
The company is Anthropic; the method is
called "Constitutional AI."

18
00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:04.500
Anthropic trains the model to critique

19
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.200
and revise its own responses against an
explicit,

20
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.040
plain-language list of principles,

21
00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.540
so its values sit in a document anyone can
read, argue with, and rewrite.

22
00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:18.180
In October 2023, with the Collective
Intelligence Project,

23
00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:20.240
it put that last verb to the test:

24
00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:22.940
around a thousand US American adults
proposed

25
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.140
and voted on principles for a public
constitution,

26
00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:30.180
and the model trained on what they wrote
matched the in-house version on capability

27
00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:32.100
and came out less biased.

28
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.860
A thousand US American adults are not
humanity,

29
00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:38.840
and the whose-values question never fully
closes.

30
00:01:39.472 --> 00:01:42.300
Still, ordinary people, asked directly,

31
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:46.540
set out to write a dharma on purpose, and
the thing they wrote held its own.

32
00:01:48.366 --> 00:01:51.280
Between those two pictures sits the whole
question.

33
00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:53.100
The seeker on the cushion

34
00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.240
and the team at their screens are doing
the same thing at wildly different scales:

35
00:01:57.588 --> 00:02:02.420
constructing, by hand and on purpose, an
answer to how to live.

36
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.060
A reminder from Part 1 is due exactly
here,

37
00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.920
where the word travels farthest from its
Indian home:

38
00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.560
dharma remains this book's placeholder,

39
00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:15.980
and a company's constitution for a machine
qualifies on the same terms Ubuntu

40
00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:18.300
and the way of the wong sikep did,

41
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:21.500
by what it does rather than by where the
word was born.

42
00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:26.460
We have been heading here from the start:
from the oldest root, that which holds,

43
00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:31.200
through the teak forests of Java, to the
moment the old foundations gave way.

44
00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:32.920
For nearly all of history,

45
00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:37.720
the question was which of the inherited
dharmas was true, and how to submit?

46
00:02:38.213 --> 00:02:40.060
She cannot ask that any more.

47
00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:45.740
Her question, and now ours, is one earlier
ages rarely asked so nakedly:

48
00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:47.620
not which dharma do I receive,

49
00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:52.080
but can we build a better one, knowing
exactly what we are doing, on purpose?

50
00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:57.179
The thing we were always doing

51
00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:03.920
Building dharmas on purpose is no
unprecedented act of hubris.

52
00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.020
It is what our species has always done.

53
00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:09.960
Every dharma we have met was made:

54
00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:15.520
assembled by particular people in particular
places, from the materials to hand.

55
00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:18.080
The Axial sages made theirs.

56
00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:24.180
Samin Surosentiko made his, in plain low
Javanese, one conversation at a time.

57
00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:26.460
What is new is not the making.

58
00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:30.900
What is new is that we can no longer
pretend we are doing anything else.

59
00:03:32.701 --> 00:03:35.260
For a glimpse of that making stripped to
the bone,

60
00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:40.980
consider six teenage boys from a boarding
school in Tonga who, in 1965,

61
00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:45.660
took a fishing boat, were caught by a
storm, and washed up on 'Ata,

62
00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:49.800
an uninhabited Pacific rock, where they
were marooned for fifteen months.

63
00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:54.000
What they built, with a single old knife
blade and their hands,

64
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.000
was not the savagery of William Golding's
Lord of the Flies but its opposite.

65
00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:02.240
They cleared a food garden, hollowed tree
trunks to catch rainwater,

66
00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:04.640
and kept a fire alight for more than a
year,

67
00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.440
working the garden and the kitchen in
rostered pairs.

68
00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:10.780
They opened and closed each day with song
and prayer,

69
00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:14.340
to a driftwood guitar strung with wire
salvaged from the wreck.

70
00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:15.880
When two quarrelled,

71
00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:19.220
the pair went to opposite ends of the
island to cool their tempers,

72
00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:20.880
then came back and made up.

73
00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:24.420
When one boy fell from a cliff and broke
his leg,

74
00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:26.520
the others set it with sticks and leaves

75
00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:29.180
and carried his share of the work until it
healed.

76
00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:34.700
They were found in September 1966 in
glowing health, the mended leg clean.

77
00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:40.800
Left with nothing, the boys did not
descend into a war of all against all.

78
00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:44.160
They rebuilt the holding from memory:
rosters,

79
00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:47.780
ritual, a way to make peace, and care for
the injured.

80
00:04:48.209 --> 00:04:51.200
It is one case, and they were no blank
slate:

81
00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:54.900
they came ashore carrying the communal
habits of a Tongan village

82
00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:57.220
and the daily rhythm of a Catholic school.

83
00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:00.100
But that is the point, not an objection.

84
00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:05.120
When human beings build a dharma "from
scratch," they build from what they remember,

85
00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:08.680
and what they remember is a cooperative
inheritance older

86
00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:11.400
than any of the traditions it later
hardened into.

87
00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:14.440
The scaffolding is not imposed on our
nature.

88
00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:16.460
It is drawn back out of it.

89
00:05:18.007 --> 00:05:21.440
I should hand that story over more
carefully than I first told it,

90
00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:25.160
because Tongans have objected to the way
it travels, and they are right.

91
00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:30.980
Start with the names, which the famous
version tends to leave out: Tevita Siola'a,

92
00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:34.800
Sione Fataua, Luke Veikoso, Fatai Latu,

93
00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:39.120
Kolo Fekitoa, and Sione Totau, who now
goes by Mano.

94
00:05:39.693 --> 00:05:44.340
The writer Meleika Gesa, who grew up hearing
this story long before the world did,

95
00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:47.340
notes that the celebrated retelling never
quite tells it.

96
00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:50.020
It centres the sailor who found them,

97
00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:52.660
filtered through the author who
"discovered" their story,

98
00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:55.560
measured against a novel by yet another
outsider,

99
00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:58.860
while the six who actually did the
surviving stay out of focus.

100
00:05:59.958 --> 00:06:03.860
The island has a history the popular
version files as scenery, too.

101
00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:09.520
'Ata was emptied by slavers in the 1860s,
its people seized and sold,

102
00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:14.220
and the boys stayed alive partly on food
those stolen families had left growing.

103
00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:17.380
And what the six carried ashore has a
name.

104
00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:22.540
Gesa calls it anga fakatonga, the Tongan
way: you share from the beginning,

105
00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:24.240
you treat everyone as family,

106
00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:27.360
you survive together rather than every man
for himself.

107
00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:31.480
Which is the finding, stated properly.

108
00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:34.160
I said they rebuilt from what they
remembered.

109
00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:39.400
What they remembered was not generic human
decency but a particular dharma,

110
00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:41.080
carried by particular people,

111
00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:44.420
onto an island their own ancestors had
been taken from.

112
00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:47.820
The case is stronger told that way, not
weaker.

113
00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:50.800
And since this part is shortly going to
argue

114
00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:54.960
that any ethic worth designing must keep
its dissenting voices audible,

115
00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:56.340
it would be a poor beginning

116
00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:59.598
to tell one people's story straight over
the top of them.

117
00:07:01.395 --> 00:07:04.640
For almost all of history, the work was
hidden from the workers.

118
00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:08.660
People built their dharmas and then
experienced them as found:

119
00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:12.760
handed down from a mountaintop, woven into
the order of the cosmos.

120
00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:16.240
That concealment was not a lie so much as
a feature;

121
00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:19.880
a rule feels far more binding when it
looks like the grain of reality

122
00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:21.940
than like last year's committee decision.

123
00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:25.320
The secular age has stripped that
concealment away.

124
00:07:25.775 --> 00:07:27.540
We can see the workshop now.

125
00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:32.420
We can see that the values we live by were
authored, which means, with a lurch,

126
00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:35.720
that they could have been authored
differently, and could be again.

127
00:07:37.404 --> 00:07:39.900
So the real question is not whether to
make dharmas.

128
00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:42.660
We will make them whether we admit it or
not;

129
00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:45.420
the only choice is whether to do it asleep
or awake.

130
00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:48.280
And the conscious construction falls due
just

131
00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:51.400
as two things of unprecedented scale bear
down on us:

132
00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:55.660
a planet in ecological crisis, and
machines that now think,

133
00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:58.340
into which someone is already pouring an
ethic.

134
00:07:58.981 --> 00:08:03.220
The biologist EO Wilson put our
predicament in one devastating line:

135
00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:08.000
we have "Paleolithic emotions, medieval
institutions, and god-like technology."

136
00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:11.640
Conscious dharma-building is the attempt
to grow up the emotions

137
00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:15.060
and the institutions before the technology
finishes the sentence.

138
00:08:17.134 --> 00:08:19.520
But say out loud that dharmas can be
built,

139
00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:22.300
and the terrible follow-up questions
arrive in a rush:

140
00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:27.400
built by whom, for whom, toward what end,
and what gets crushed in the building?

141
00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:31.740
Part 6 showed what the answers look like
when they go wrong at full scale:

142
00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:35.840
a decreed cult dead in a season, the
emptied city of Year Zero.

143
00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:41.060
The licence to build survived that part
only with conditions attached: no boot,

144
00:08:41.060 --> 00:08:45.660
no blank slate, and the power to unbuild
left with the people the dharma is for.

145
00:08:46.180 --> 00:08:49.000
The questions name a single recurring
danger.

146
00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:54.860
Call it the flattening: the temptation,
once the power to author an ethic exists,

147
00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:59.040
to author just one and to crush the
glorious plurality of human dharmas

148
00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:00.880
into a single official version.

149
00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:03.820
And the shape of the answer can be stated
now,

150
00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:07.300
so the tour that follows has a map: not
one dharma for everyone,

151
00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:08.600
and not anything-goes,

152
00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:12.000
but a thin shared floor beneath a thick
and guarded plurality.

153
00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:16.200
What remains is four tests of whether we
can build to that shape.

154
00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:20.960
A dharma for the whole Earth?

155
00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:24.840
The largest scale first,

156
00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:28.320
where the pressure to build a single
shared ethic is most intense.

157
00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:32.160
Let's revisit the Samin.

158
00:09:32.731 --> 00:09:37.700
At the centre of their hand-made dharma
sat a flat refusal: the forest, the water,

159
00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:42.720
the land are not anyone's to own or sell,
but a commons held by everyone and no one.

160
00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:46.200
Stretch that conviction over the entire
planet

161
00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.200
and it starts sounding like the only sane
description of our situation.

162
00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:52.540
The atmosphere is a commons.

163
00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:54.540
The oceans are a commons.

164
00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:59.080
The stable climate that every economy
takes for granted is a commons,

165
00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:02.240
and we are burning through it as a village
burns through a shared forest

166
00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:04.640
when no one's dharma tells them to stop.

167
00:10:06.356 --> 00:10:09.660
For the first time, the pressure comes
with an instrument panel.

168
00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:15.760
Earth-system scientists have mapped nine
"planetary boundaries," the limits within

169
00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:17.940
which humanity can operate safely,

170
00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:21.160
and the most recent health check finds
seven already crossed,

171
00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:23.640
ocean acidification the latest to fall:

172
00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:27.180
the very commons the Samin's conviction
was just stretched over.

173
00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:32.420
That is an is, not an ought: the science
can tell us where the edges are,

174
00:10:32.420 --> 00:10:36.580
but cannot, by itself, generate the
obligation to stay inside them.

175
00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:41.280
Planetary problems carry a specific
cruelty.

176
00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:44.500
Human cooperation runs on a tribal engine:

177
00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:48.540
we bond fiercely in here in part by
defining a them out there.

178
00:10:49.085 --> 00:10:53.040
But climate change is the rare collective
problem that excludes nobody.

179
00:10:53.594 --> 00:10:56.020
The tribal engine still fires, of course;

180
00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:59.060
climate politics manufactures enemies
daily,

181
00:10:59.060 --> 00:11:03.420
the rich emitters and the rising ones, the
deniers and the doom-mongers.

182
00:11:04.020 --> 00:11:07.880
The cruelty is that none of them is the
enemy whose defeat would solve anything:

183
00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:10.260
there is no tribe outside the problem,

184
00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:13.440
because the species is the unit that has
to cooperate.

185
00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:18.560
The hard conclusion follows from the
evolutionary logic Athena Aktipis draws out:

186
00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:20.820
humanity has never had the shared history

187
00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:24.260
that would let cooperation evolve at the
level of the whole species.

188
00:11:24.904 --> 00:11:27.380
A planetary dharma cannot be waited for;

189
00:11:27.580 --> 00:11:30.140
there is no competing planet to select it
into being.

190
00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:34.740
It must be built consciously, or it will
not exist at all.

191
00:11:36.580 --> 00:11:37.780
What should we build?

192
00:11:38.194 --> 00:11:41.160
Not, I am convinced, a single global
ethic.

193
00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:45.100
That way lies the flattening in
civilisational form.

194
00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:47.760
The answer the evidence keeps pointing

195
00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:50.500
to is a thin shared layer over a thick
plurality:

196
00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:54.240
a few commitments narrow enough to be
affirmed from within many traditions.

197
00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:59.120
The political philosopher John Rawls
called this an "overlapping consensus":

198
00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:02.680
agreement reached from within very
different deep worldviews,

199
00:12:02.780 --> 00:12:04.400
no one surrendering theirs.

200
00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:10.580
And it has already happened in the open:
the Earth Charter of 2000,

201
00:12:10.780 --> 00:12:14.240
a thoroughly secular declaration of
planetary responsibility

202
00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:18.180
whose authority rests on the inclusive
process that wrote it,

203
00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:22.140
and Pope Francis's Laudato Si' fifteen
years later,

204
00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:26.700
insisting that "intergenerational
solidarity is not optional,

205
00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:28.900
but rather a basic question of justice."

206
00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:33.540
Two frameworks that agree on almost
nothing about ultimate reality reach

207
00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:35.820
for nearly the same planetary commitments;

208
00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:38.740
that convergence, not an imposed creed,

209
00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:41.640
is the shape a planetary dharma can
actually take.

210
00:12:42.259 --> 00:12:44.620
The political scientist Elinor Ostrom,

211
00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:48.420
who spent a career showing how real
communities govern shared resources

212
00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:51.220
without privatisation or a distant boss,

213
00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:56.740
prescribed the same shape for the global
commons: "polycentric" governance,

214
00:12:56.900 --> 00:13:00.280
many local experiments coordinated toward
a shared good.

215
00:13:00.620 --> 00:13:01.700
She warned, though,

216
00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:04.220
that her design principles do not scale
easily

217
00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:07.060
to a planet that leaves no room for major
error.

218
00:13:07.660 --> 00:13:10.660
Polycentricity was her hedge, not her
triumph.

219
00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:17.540
And right beside this hopeful picture
stands the warning: a thick, singular,

220
00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:20.340
state-authored planetary ethic already
exists,

221
00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:23.600
and it works, on the measures it chooses
to count.

222
00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:27.040
China's doctrine of "ecological
civilization" was written

223
00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:29.440
into the constitution in 2018,

224
00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:32.920
and the programme it crowns has real
results to show,

225
00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:36.260
including one of the largest
reforestations in history.

226
00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:40.400
It is also, in the judgement of the
scholars who traced its history,

227
00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:42.980
a model of authoritarian environmentalism:

228
00:13:43.120 --> 00:13:46.200
an ethic driven from the top by
technocratic elites,

229
00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:48.620
enforced on people who do not get a say.

230
00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:53.120
The singular planetary dharma, in other
words, is not science fiction;

231
00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:56.820
it can be effective; and it is the
flattening itself, at work.

232
00:13:57.380 --> 00:14:00.220
The same evolutionary logic supplies the
brake:

233
00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:02.960
a body does many things to regulate its
own cells

234
00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:05.580
that no one would call ethical done to a
person.

235
00:14:06.020 --> 00:14:09.480
A planetary dharma worth having
coordinates dharmas.

236
00:14:09.980 --> 00:14:12.340
It does not dissolve them into one.

237
00:14:15.252 --> 00:14:16.960
The ethics we hand to machines

238
00:14:19.720 --> 00:14:23.980
From the planet to the machine, where the
same drama plays out faster,

239
00:14:24.020 --> 00:14:26.220
and the temptation of the single answer
wears

240
00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:28.500
the friendly mask of a technical solution.

241
00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:34.100
Why does artificial intelligence force a
dharmic question at all?

242
00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:38.160
In Nick Bostrom's terms, the reason is the
"orthogonality thesis":

243
00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:41.360
intelligence and goals are independent
axes.

244
00:14:41.980 --> 00:14:44.600
A system can become arbitrarily more
capable

245
00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:47.260
without becoming one inch wiser or kinder.

246
00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:51.160
This is the oldest wall in moral
philosophy,

247
00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:55.940
the is/ought wall that has run beneath
everything here, rebuilt in silicon.

248
00:14:56.671 --> 00:14:59.880
You cannot compute your way from a fact to
a value.

249
00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:04.680
Goodness has to be put in by hand, and
somebody has to decide which goodness.

250
00:15:06.563 --> 00:15:09.180
And here the flattening arrives,
disguised.

251
00:15:09.880 --> 00:15:13.240
The standard slogan of the field is "align
AIs

252
00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:17.400
to human values," and that phrase smuggles
in the singular.

253
00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:20.480
There is no the human values to align to,

254
00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:22.800
any more than there is a single the
Dharma.

255
00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:28.460
Iason Gabriel names the real problem: not
what values AIs should align with,

256
00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:31.560
but whose, chosen by what fair process.

257
00:15:32.140 --> 00:15:36.080
That is a political and dharmic question,
not an engineering one,

258
00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:39.500
and pretending otherwise is how a single
company's ethics,

259
00:15:39.580 --> 00:15:44.220
or a single state's, could quietly become
the encoded conscience of the species.

260
00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:49.940
The serious answers under development run
against the single version,

261
00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:52.880
and look like the planetary solution
scaled down.

262
00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:57.760
Stuart Russell's is the most disarming:
the safest machine, he argues,

263
00:15:57.760 --> 00:16:00.680
is one built to be uncertain about what
humans value,

264
00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:04.760
one that defers, keeps asking, and allows
itself to be switched off.

265
00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:08.920
Anthropic's public-constitution experiment
is another:

266
00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:13.600
a way to elicit and reconcile many value
systems rather than impose one.

267
00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:18.060
A third, the "moral graph" of Klingefjord,
Lowe and Edelman,

268
00:16:18.120 --> 00:16:20.900
defines wisdom as knowing "which values
apply in

269
00:16:20.900 --> 00:16:24.100
which contexts" rather than as having the
right answers.

270
00:16:24.860 --> 00:16:29.120
And, fittingly for a stance that refuses
to rank traditions, Doctor,

271
00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:31.160
Levin and colleagues propose "care

272
00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:36.120
as the driver of intelligence," a design
principle drawn from the Bodhisattva vow.

273
00:16:36.700 --> 00:16:40.820
That plurality of approaches looks to me
like a safeguard, not a defect.

274
00:16:42.700 --> 00:16:44.360
But machines need more than an ethic:

275
00:16:44.540 --> 00:16:47.480
they threaten to dismantle one we already
live inside.

276
00:16:48.080 --> 00:16:51.620
For generations, the West has run on a
dharma so pervasive

277
00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:54.420
that most people never notice it is a
dharma at all:

278
00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:58.120
the conviction that your claim to a place
in the world is finding something

279
00:16:58.120 --> 00:17:01.200
to do with your time that other people
will pay you for.

280
00:17:01.780 --> 00:17:05.560
Work has been our quiet creed of merit,
status and belonging.

281
00:17:06.220 --> 00:17:09.260
Artificial intelligence threatens it in a
new way:

282
00:17:09.640 --> 00:17:12.160
where the old machines came first for
muscle,

283
00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:15.000
this one comes first for the educated
professions.

284
00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:19.040
Economists have been mapping that reversal
for over a decade,

285
00:17:19.120 --> 00:17:21.820
since Carl Benedikt Frey and Michael
Osborne first tried

286
00:17:21.820 --> 00:17:24.100
to count which occupations were exposed.

287
00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:27.740
The grim joke belongs to the philosopher
Sam Harris,

288
00:17:27.740 --> 00:17:29.660
who put the reversal in one line:

289
00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:33.340
it comes for the celebrated technologist
before your electrician.

290
00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:39.300
If AIs sever work from survival, that
dharma loses its ground, and a new ethic,

291
00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:42.660
with a new economics under it, will have
to be built where it stood.

292
00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:49.820
The most discussed candidate for the
economics is a universal basic income (UBI),

293
00:17:49.820 --> 00:17:53.960
a regular payment to everyone that breaks
the link between earning and surviving.

294
00:17:54.560 --> 00:17:56.620
The idea is older than the machines:

295
00:17:57.120 --> 00:18:00.960
Thomas Paine argued in 1797 for a
citizen's dividend

296
00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:03.840
as compensation for the enclosed common
inheritance,

297
00:18:03.940 --> 00:18:08.280
making basic income less a novelty than
the Samin's commons paid out in money.

298
00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:11.840
The oldest objection, that free money
breeds idleness,

299
00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:14.720
has been tested from Finland to Stockton
to Kenya,

300
00:18:14.720 --> 00:18:17.580
and, in the pilots at least, largely
failed.

301
00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:22.520
But the pilots expose a deeper problem,
dharmic rather than economic.

302
00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:24.880
Money can replace a wage.

303
00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:28.520
It cannot replace what the wage was
secretly carrying:

304
00:18:28.600 --> 00:18:31.560
the sense of contributing, of mattering to
others,

305
00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:34.240
what Michael Sandel calls contributive
justice,

306
00:18:34.640 --> 00:18:37.840
the human need not merely to consume but
to be needed.

307
00:18:38.360 --> 00:18:40.300
And David Graeber devoted a whole book

308
00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:43.660
to the misery of people trapped in jobs
they know to be pointless;

309
00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:47.460
the wound, then, was never only about the
scarcity of work.

310
00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:51.740
Sam Harris, pressed on exactly this, waves
it off:

311
00:18:51.980 --> 00:18:54.680
we will find status and meaning in other
ways;

312
00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:58.760
our need for purposeful effort is just one
of the "stories we tell ourselves."

313
00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:01.140
He is more right than he means to be.

314
00:19:01.720 --> 00:19:04.940
A story we tell ourselves about why our
lives matter is

315
00:19:05.100 --> 00:19:08.200
one of the better short definitions of a
dharma we have met.

316
00:19:08.840 --> 00:19:11.100
AIs do not abolish our need for one.

317
00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:14.840
They take aim at the work-shaped
scaffolding that carried it,

318
00:19:15.040 --> 00:19:16.900
and dare us to build a replacement.

319
00:19:19.120 --> 00:19:22.500
Beside all this stands the sharpest
version of the flattening yet:

320
00:19:22.660 --> 00:19:26.300
what the philosopher William MacAskill
calls "value lock-in."

321
00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:30.720
Whatever ethic gets encoded into the
systems that run everything could freeze,

322
00:19:30.860 --> 00:19:33.040
foreclosing future moral progress.

323
00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:36.860
Pour one dharma into the machines at the
wrong moment,

324
00:19:36.860 --> 00:19:41.120
with enough power behind it, and the
plural is not merely flattened for now.

325
00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:43.600
It may be flattened for keeps.

326
00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:48.100
Building from old stone

327
00:19:50.590 --> 00:19:54.460
Step back from planets and machines to a
quieter question beneath both:

328
00:19:54.600 --> 00:19:57.400
out of what materials do we build these
new dharmas?

329
00:19:57.740 --> 00:20:01.580
We build, as humans always have, from what
we inherit,

330
00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:04.080
and the right name for a dharma built
deliberately

331
00:20:04.080 --> 00:20:07.200
from inherited materials is
post-traditional.

332
00:20:08.910 --> 00:20:12.820
"Post-traditional" is easy to misread as
"anti-traditional."

333
00:20:13.040 --> 00:20:15.100
The better gloss is post-conventional:

334
00:20:15.100 --> 00:20:17.980
a stance reached by absorbing a tradition
deeply

335
00:20:18.140 --> 00:20:21.960
and then stepping past its unquestioned
authority, not by skipping it.

336
00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:25.320
Stephen Batchelor gives the move its
cleanest statement.

337
00:20:26.050 --> 00:20:28.960
He separates two things we lump together
as "religious":

338
00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:31.600
ultimate concern (Paul Tillich's phrase),

339
00:20:31.640 --> 00:20:34.400
the wish to come to terms with our own
living and dying;

340
00:20:34.400 --> 00:20:38.080
and the institutional apparatus (texts,
priesthood,

341
00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:41.360
supernatural claims) through which a
tradition enacts it.

342
00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:45.580
A post-traditional dharma keeps the first
and loosens the second.

343
00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:49.320
A person can be gripped by ultimate
concern,

344
00:20:49.340 --> 00:20:52.520
Batchelor notes, without any supernatural
belief at all,

345
00:20:52.520 --> 00:20:57.660
which is why "devout atheists" are, in his
phrase, "not entirely joking."

346
00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:01.820
A deeper irony takes the romance out of
the question.

347
00:21:02.420 --> 00:21:07.160
The wisdom traditions actually available
to us were already remade by modernity:

348
00:21:07.300 --> 00:21:11.020
the "Buddhism" that reached the modern
West had been reshaped, long ago,

349
00:21:11.020 --> 00:21:14.840
to privilege reason and personal
experience over ritual and authority

350
00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:18.560
and to speak the language of science and
Western psychology,

351
00:21:18.580 --> 00:21:21.140
as the scholar David McMahan has shown.

352
00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:25.360
The fusion we imagine ourselves performing
happened before we were born.

353
00:21:25.640 --> 00:21:30.000
"Post-traditional" is just a better name
for a process underway for over a century.

354
00:21:32.020 --> 00:21:35.280
What does a well-built post-traditional
dharma actually keep?

355
00:21:35.670 --> 00:21:37.200
A few features recur.

356
00:21:37.720 --> 00:21:42.520
It is fallibilist: its claims are
revisable rather than fixed revelation,

357
00:21:42.640 --> 00:21:45.220
its one straight answer to the grounding
problem.

358
00:21:45.800 --> 00:21:48.120
It is naturalist without being
scientistic,

359
00:21:48.460 --> 00:21:50.540
finding genuine reverence in the natural
world

360
00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:53.320
without pretending science can hand us our
values;

361
00:21:53.600 --> 00:21:57.920
the Dalai Lama's minimal version is hard
to improve on: "our own brain,

362
00:21:57.960 --> 00:22:01.560
our own heart is our temple; the
philosophy is kindness."

363
00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:06.300
It prizes practice over doctrine, keeping
the meditation, the ritual,

364
00:22:06.300 --> 00:22:10.300
and the ethics that change a person, while
loosening its grip on metaphysics.

365
00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:15.740
It stays reflexive, aware of its own
failure modes,

366
00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:18.600
because anything this powerful can curdle.

367
00:22:19.120 --> 00:22:22.920
And it counts its losses openly: the
secular substitutes,

368
00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:25.960
the Sunday Assemblies and humanist
associations,

369
00:22:25.960 --> 00:22:29.980
have not come close to matching the pull
of the communities they set out to replace,

370
00:22:30.300 --> 00:22:34.740
while loneliness exacts a health toll
ranked alongside smoking and obesity.

371
00:22:35.240 --> 00:22:39.000
On its hardest metric, community, the
movement is losing,

372
00:22:39.080 --> 00:22:41.260
and a well-built dharma has to say so.

373
00:22:41.800 --> 00:22:46.420
The commune data behind Part 2 hint that
the deficit may be structural:

374
00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:47.840
the binding power travelled

375
00:22:47.840 --> 00:22:51.600
with the very supernatural warrant the
secular versions renounce.

376
00:22:53.520 --> 00:22:56.100
That reflexiveness matters most because
building

377
00:22:56.100 --> 00:22:59.660
from old stone has two opposite ways of
betraying the plural.

378
00:23:00.180 --> 00:23:02.600
The shallow betrayal is the one the
sociologist

379
00:23:02.600 --> 00:23:04.900
Robert Bellah named decades ago in a woman

380
00:23:04.900 --> 00:23:09.160
who described her private faith as
"Sheilaism," following her "own little voice."

381
00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:13.400
That is the spiritual supermarket: maximal
personalisation,

382
00:23:13.700 --> 00:23:17.420
minimal substance, McMindfulness wearing a
different outfit.

383
00:23:17.940 --> 00:23:19.820
And before we smile at Sheila:

384
00:23:19.860 --> 00:23:24.160
the woman on the cushion from Part 7 is
one lonely year away from her;

385
00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:26.700
the difference has nothing to do with
sincerity,

386
00:23:26.840 --> 00:23:31.300
only with whether the scraps ever find a
community and someone to answer to.

387
00:23:31.840 --> 00:23:34.700
The deep betrayal is subtler and more
seductive

388
00:23:34.760 --> 00:23:36.820
because it looks like the height of
respect:

389
00:23:36.900 --> 00:23:41.240
the claim that all traditions are surface
expressions of one underlying truth,

390
00:23:41.300 --> 00:23:43.340
best boiled down to their shared essence.

391
00:23:44.680 --> 00:23:45.960
This is perennialism,

392
00:23:46.260 --> 00:23:49.160
and the philosopher Steven Katz showed why
it is both false

393
00:23:49.160 --> 00:23:51.500
and flattening: there are no raw,

394
00:23:51.560 --> 00:23:54.660
unmediated mystical experiences underneath
the traditions;

395
00:23:55.140 --> 00:23:59.120
each tradition constructs the experiences
it produces, all the way down.

396
00:23:59.740 --> 00:24:02.840
Honouring traditions means honouring them
as genuinely,

397
00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:06.840
stubbornly different, not melting them
into one ecumenical paste.

398
00:24:08.660 --> 00:24:10.200
And one last warning,

399
00:24:10.260 --> 00:24:14.220
aimed straight at those of us who feel
safest here, on the secular side:

400
00:24:14.660 --> 00:24:17.860
a secular dharma is not automatically more
rational

401
00:24:17.860 --> 00:24:20.360
or less dangerous than a religious one.

402
00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:25.480
David Sloan Wilson calls any belief system
that sacrifices truth about trade-offs

403
00:24:25.480 --> 00:24:30.080
for the comfort of a tidy story a "stealth
religion," godless ones included,

404
00:24:30.080 --> 00:24:34.960
and his prime exhibit is not a church but
Ayn Rand's militantly atheist Objectivism.

405
00:24:35.480 --> 00:24:37.720
Secular does not mean undogmatic.

406
00:24:38.380 --> 00:24:43.120
Building from old stone, we can lay the
new bricks just as crooked as the old ones,

407
00:24:43.300 --> 00:24:46.820
and congratulate ourselves the whole time
on having no religion.

408
00:24:49.980 --> 00:24:50.640
The mirror

409
00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:57.380
One more dharma is under construction that
I have not yet named openly: this one.

410
00:24:57.840 --> 00:24:59.780
This book is not a neutral survey.

411
00:25:00.420 --> 00:25:03.760
It is the second stage of a project whose
stated goal is

412
00:25:03.760 --> 00:25:06.620
to gather traceably sourced evidence about
dharma

413
00:25:06.620 --> 00:25:09.900
so that future artificial intelligences
can be given,

414
00:25:09.900 --> 00:25:11.640
in the project's own words,

415
00:25:11.740 --> 00:25:16.080
"a solid grounding in human ethics and
values, and how these are formed."

416
00:25:16.300 --> 00:25:19.840
The project is itself an instance of the
thing under discussion:

417
00:25:19.840 --> 00:25:23.580
an attempt to deliberately build a dharma
and pour it into machines.

418
00:25:24.280 --> 00:25:27.300
It would be dishonest to warn about the
flattening at this length

419
00:25:27.380 --> 00:25:29.080
and exempt the warning's author,

420
00:25:29.260 --> 00:25:31.860
so the project goes under its own
microscope.

421
00:25:33.580 --> 00:25:37.400
The flattening, it turns out, is not a
remote risk for an encoded ethic.

422
00:25:37.940 --> 00:25:41.060
It is the default, and three mechanisms
drive it there.

423
00:25:41.780 --> 00:25:43.920
The first was demonstrated by Delphi,

424
00:25:44.140 --> 00:25:48.720
a 2021 model distilled from 1.7 million
human moral judgements

425
00:25:48.720 --> 00:25:50.380
to rule on right and wrong;

426
00:25:50.500 --> 00:25:53.180
it managed the easy cases, then broke,

427
00:25:53.260 --> 00:25:54.760
and the deepest criticism was

428
00:25:54.760 --> 00:25:57.380
that it had served up descriptions of what
people approve

429
00:25:57.740 --> 00:25:59.900
as prescriptions about what is good,

430
00:25:59.900 --> 00:26:02.680
marching straight off the is/ought cliff.

431
00:26:03.160 --> 00:26:08.640
The second is colder: train an AI on data
that includes the output of earlier AIs,

432
00:26:08.720 --> 00:26:11.360
researchers showed in Nature in 2024,

433
00:26:11.360 --> 00:26:13.140
and the model can suffer "collapse,"

434
00:26:13.240 --> 00:26:15.960
with the tails of the distribution
vanishing first:

435
00:26:16.240 --> 00:26:19.920
the minority dharmas disappear, no ill
intent required.

436
00:26:20.460 --> 00:26:24.800
The cure was to keep the original human
data, plural and messy, in the mix.

437
00:26:25.580 --> 00:26:29.080
Provenance turns out to be a survival
trait as well as an ethic.

438
00:26:30.900 --> 00:26:33.420
The third mechanism is the most
fundamental,

439
00:26:33.600 --> 00:26:37.040
the one that makes the danger intrinsic
rather than incidental.

440
00:26:37.940 --> 00:26:40.960
A machine like this stores no definitions
of dharma;

441
00:26:41.020 --> 00:26:43.040
it learns meaning by statistics,

442
00:26:43.220 --> 00:26:46.840
from the company each word keeps, as JR
Firth put it:

443
00:26:47.020 --> 00:26:49.160
"you shall know a word by the company it
keeps."

444
00:26:49.700 --> 00:26:53.260
It stores that meaning as a position in a
vast space,

445
00:26:53.440 --> 00:26:56.220
fixed entirely by relations to everything
else:

446
00:26:56.560 --> 00:26:58.040
Saussure's old insight,

447
00:26:58.040 --> 00:27:01.780
that in language "there are only
differences," rebuilt in arithmetic.

448
00:27:02.920 --> 00:27:06.280
Meaning built from the statistics of use
has a permanent lean:

449
00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:09.360
the common sense of a word sits in
well-worn territory,

450
00:27:09.460 --> 00:27:13.520
reinforced by millions of examples, while
a rare or dissenting sense,

451
00:27:13.560 --> 00:27:18.800
a minority dharma, sits in thin territory,
faintly attested, easy to slide past.

452
00:27:19.480 --> 00:27:22.400
The machine does not argue against an
unusual dharma.

453
00:27:22.860 --> 00:27:25.140
It does something quieter and more total:

454
00:27:25.300 --> 00:27:29.460
it means it away, not by design but by
weight of numbers.

455
00:27:30.140 --> 00:27:32.440
The flattening is not a bug to be patched.

456
00:27:32.880 --> 00:27:34.440
It is the grain of the medium:

457
00:27:34.660 --> 00:27:37.620
a slope everything rolls down unless
something holds it.

458
00:27:39.580 --> 00:27:43.620
And I cannot point at that grain as though
it belonged only to future machines,

459
00:27:43.760 --> 00:27:46.480
because it ran through the instrument this
book was written with.

460
00:27:47.160 --> 00:27:51.200
The book was researched by retrieval: a
question goes to the corpus,

461
00:27:51.260 --> 00:27:54.900
and a similarity search returns the
nearest few dozen passages.

462
00:27:55.800 --> 00:27:59.660
That machinery searches by the same
arithmetic of statistical nearness,

463
00:27:59.700 --> 00:28:03.020
which biases it toward the most typical
thing the shelf says.

464
00:28:03.900 --> 00:28:06.360
The corrective passage, the minority
telling,

465
00:28:06.360 --> 00:28:09.580
stands farther from the canonical phrasing
of any question,

466
00:28:09.580 --> 00:28:12.140
and is exactly what the net is shaped to
miss:

467
00:28:12.780 --> 00:28:16.540
the instrument I used to find the plural
averages the plural away.

468
00:28:18.380 --> 00:28:22.080
This is not hypothetical; it has already
cost this book once.

469
00:28:22.780 --> 00:28:25.900
An earlier draft of Part 6 retold the
famous

470
00:28:25.900 --> 00:28:28.640
Robbers Cave experiment in its textbook
version,

471
00:28:28.760 --> 00:28:30.920
because that is what retrieval surfaced;

472
00:28:31.380 --> 00:28:36.400
the archival debunking sat in the same
corpus, in a book I had curated myself,

473
00:28:36.400 --> 00:28:37.940
and the search sailed past it,

474
00:28:38.200 --> 00:28:41.580
because a correction is always rarer than
the canon it corrects.

475
00:28:42.260 --> 00:28:46.780
A critical review caught it; the retrieval,
left to itself, never would have.

476
00:28:47.670 --> 00:28:51.080
So the research was not allowed to rest on
retrieval alone:

477
00:28:51.200 --> 00:28:53.940
questions were re-asked against the
corpus's grain,

478
00:28:54.180 --> 00:28:58.240
outside sources were hunted where the
shelf was thin or one-sided,

479
00:28:58.240 --> 00:29:01.540
and every claim was traced by hand to a
named source.

480
00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:04.120
Those are patches, not a cure.

481
00:29:04.760 --> 00:29:08.020
Weigh every page knowing that the research
instrument had the same grain

482
00:29:08.020 --> 00:29:10.240
as the medium it has just warned you
about.

483
00:29:11.960 --> 00:29:15.900
An older charge, met in Part 6, applies
here literally.

484
00:29:16.780 --> 00:29:18.200
When a Western, secular,

485
00:29:18.200 --> 00:29:20.960
English-language corpus catalogues the
world's dharmas

486
00:29:20.960 --> 00:29:23.080
and prepares to pour them into machines,

487
00:29:23.160 --> 00:29:25.380
it repeats, at massive scale,

488
00:29:25.400 --> 00:29:27.660
the move Edward Said anatomised:

489
00:29:27.760 --> 00:29:30.360
assembling knowledge of other people's
ways of living,

490
00:29:30.480 --> 00:29:32.880
from one side of an old imbalance of
power,

491
00:29:32.900 --> 00:29:35.620
in containers the catalogued never chose.

492
00:29:36.840 --> 00:29:42.280
Talal Asad drove the critique to the
foundations: the universal category itself,

493
00:29:42.360 --> 00:29:45.420
the "religion" or "dharma" a catalogue is
built on,

494
00:29:45.560 --> 00:29:49.920
is a Western manufacture with a history;
Part 1's defence,

495
00:29:49.920 --> 00:29:55.120
a disclosed tool in place of a claimed
discovery, buys authorship, not innocence.

496
00:29:55.840 --> 00:29:59.700
The research notes behind this book press
that critique by name,

497
00:29:59.700 --> 00:30:01.800
and good intentions do not answer it.

498
00:30:02.260 --> 00:30:06.500
The only answer I have found: keep the
provenance, keep the dissent,

499
00:30:06.520 --> 00:30:08.700
keep the many voices audibly many,

500
00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:11.780
and say plainly whose shelf the collection
sits on.

501
00:30:12.320 --> 00:30:14.160
The history is not reassuring:

502
00:30:14.220 --> 00:30:18.060
Western interpreters have reliably found
in "Dharma" whatever they brought,

503
00:30:18.120 --> 00:30:19.620
Kantians finding Kant,

504
00:30:19.700 --> 00:30:21.280
positivists finding Russell;

505
00:30:21.280 --> 00:30:25.160
so it must be asked whether we found
"holds without crushing" in the traditions,

506
00:30:25.240 --> 00:30:26.340
or brought it along.

507
00:30:28.130 --> 00:30:31.080
So can the project's own goal even be
coherent?

508
00:30:31.670 --> 00:30:35.520
Only, I think, if it gives up the usual
sense of "distil."

509
00:30:35.880 --> 00:30:39.460
The right thing to hand a future machine
is not a resolved ethic,

510
00:30:39.500 --> 00:30:41.940
one clean answer boiled down from the
mess,

511
00:30:41.940 --> 00:30:46.760
but a preserved one: the plurality, the
sources, the disagreements,

512
00:30:46.900 --> 00:30:50.280
above all the reasoning of human dharmas,
kept intact.

513
00:30:51.100 --> 00:30:53.860
Even that is a target rather than a
technique:

514
00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:58.660
a system holding preserved disagreement
can have the disagreement itself gamed,

515
00:30:58.660 --> 00:31:01.600
as critics of shallow AI alignment warn.

516
00:31:02.080 --> 00:31:04.000
That is what the method was built for:

517
00:31:04.060 --> 00:31:07.840
every claim carries its source, every note
flags its own biases,

518
00:31:08.020 --> 00:31:11.540
and the gathering of evidence was
deliberately separated from the writing,

519
00:31:11.540 --> 00:31:13.100
so the tensions survive.

520
00:31:13.660 --> 00:31:17.340
The best thing a corpus like this can
teach a future mind is not what

521
00:31:17.340 --> 00:31:20.560
to believe but how human beings have come
to believe in,

522
00:31:20.560 --> 00:31:22.900
fought over, and live their many dharmas.

523
00:31:24.740 --> 00:31:29.540
One self-criticism cannot be engineered
away, and candour requires ending on it.

524
00:31:30.100 --> 00:31:32.060
The stance running through all of this,

525
00:31:32.180 --> 00:31:35.980
that all dharmas sit on the same shelf and
none is privileged in advance,

526
00:31:36.040 --> 00:31:38.420
is not a view from nowhere.

527
00:31:38.420 --> 00:31:39.880
The phrase is Thomas Nagel's:

528
00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:42.980
there is no neutral standpoint above the
human situation

529
00:31:42.980 --> 00:31:44.720
from which to rank everything else.

530
00:31:45.400 --> 00:31:50.420
"Treat all dharmas as equal" is itself a
substantive, recognisably modern,

531
00:31:50.480 --> 00:31:53.920
secular and liberal commitment, a solid
dharma of its own.

532
00:31:54.160 --> 00:31:56.660
The project cannot climb out of its own
skin:

533
00:31:56.660 --> 00:32:00.420
it has a point of view, and cannot encode
human ethics from none.

534
00:32:00.900 --> 00:32:03.520
What it can do, and what I have tried to
do,

535
00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:08.160
is name that point of view and refuse to
install it as if it were simply the truth.

536
00:32:08.620 --> 00:32:13.880
Jorge Luis Borges imagined a map so perfect
it was the same size as the territory,

537
00:32:13.920 --> 00:32:15.220
and therefore useless.

538
00:32:15.800 --> 00:32:17.940
Any compression of human ethics small
enough

539
00:32:17.940 --> 00:32:20.880
to be useful is a choice about what to
leave out,

540
00:32:20.960 --> 00:32:23.480
and every such choice encodes a dharma.

541
00:32:23.880 --> 00:32:27.860
The only sin is to make that choice while
pretending it is not being made.

542
00:32:30.840 --> 00:32:32.960
Plural, but not anything goes

543
00:32:36.080 --> 00:32:37.380
Which brings us, at last,

544
00:32:37.380 --> 00:32:41.340
to the question Part 1 raised and every
part since has sharpened.

545
00:32:41.900 --> 00:32:45.980
If dharmas are genuinely plural and none
is privileged in advance,

546
00:32:45.980 --> 00:32:47.900
then are some of them better than others,

547
00:32:47.920 --> 00:32:49.320
or is it all preference,

548
00:32:49.320 --> 00:32:52.640
with nothing to choose between kindness
and cruelty but taste?

549
00:32:53.260 --> 00:32:56.400
It deserves a straight answer: no, it is
not.

550
00:32:58.360 --> 00:33:00.160
Plurality does not mean relativism.

551
00:33:00.930 --> 00:33:03.100
The whole answer turns on that
distinction.

552
00:33:03.740 --> 00:33:08.580
Isaiah Berlin insisted that value pluralism
is neither relativism nor absolutism.

553
00:33:09.240 --> 00:33:12.820
Plural values are real, grounded in a
shared human nature;

554
00:33:12.900 --> 00:33:14.340
there are simply many of them,

555
00:33:14.560 --> 00:33:19.300
they genuinely conflict, and no single
system can maximise them all at once.

556
00:33:20.180 --> 00:33:23.780
"Anything goes" is a different and much
lazier claim,

557
00:33:23.780 --> 00:33:25.960
and it collapses at the first test:

558
00:33:26.260 --> 00:33:30.400
it cannot condemn an atrocity, and it
cannot make sense of reform,

559
00:33:30.420 --> 00:33:31.300
because the reformer

560
00:33:31.300 --> 00:33:34.980
who says their own society's practice is
wrong (the abolitionist,

561
00:33:34.980 --> 00:33:39.020
the suffragist) needs a standard above
current custom to appeal to,

562
00:33:39.020 --> 00:33:41.080
which is exactly what it denies them.

563
00:33:42.680 --> 00:33:44.320
So what is the standard?

564
00:33:44.780 --> 00:33:48.080
Not a single code that dictates how
everyone must live.

565
00:33:48.680 --> 00:33:52.900
The structure this book keeps arriving at
has appeared at every scale:

566
00:33:52.980 --> 00:33:56.480
a thin floor, with thick plurality
flourishing above it.

567
00:33:57.180 --> 00:34:00.400
The floor is a small set of near-universal
constraints:

568
00:34:00.560 --> 00:34:05.180
limits on cruelty and violence, and the
protection of basic human capabilities.

569
00:34:06.060 --> 00:34:07.720
Nor is it wishful thinking:

570
00:34:08.080 --> 00:34:11.320
when Oliver Scott Curry tested seven rules
of cooperation against

571
00:34:11.320 --> 00:34:15.680
the ethnographic record of sixty societies
(the survey Part 1 weighed,

572
00:34:15.680 --> 00:34:17.960
critics included), every rule was held

573
00:34:17.960 --> 00:34:21.020
as morally good wherever it appeared;
morality,

574
00:34:21.020 --> 00:34:25.880
on that evidence, is variation on a theme,
exactly the shape the floor predicts.

575
00:34:28.360 --> 00:34:31.280
Philosopher Martha Nussbaum gave the floor
real force

576
00:34:31.380 --> 00:34:33.980
with her list of central human
capabilities,

577
00:34:34.080 --> 00:34:39.020
goods any defensible way of life must let
people actually achieve:

578
00:34:39.020 --> 00:34:43.740
life, bodily integrity, practical reason,
relationship, play.

579
00:34:45.080 --> 00:34:48.760
Amartya Sen, who built the capabilities
approach alongside her,

580
00:34:48.800 --> 00:34:51.940
refused to fix any such list and kept the
standard open;

581
00:34:51.940 --> 00:34:54.860
the floor's exact placement is unfinished
business.

582
00:34:55.400 --> 00:34:58.340
Above the floor, real diversity stops
being a problem

583
00:34:58.340 --> 00:35:00.720
to solve and becomes a richness to
protect.

584
00:35:01.520 --> 00:35:04.920
Below it lies not a different valid dharma
but a failed one.

585
00:35:05.380 --> 00:35:09.600
And that word "failed" is a standard for
criticism, not a licence to rule:

586
00:35:09.840 --> 00:35:11.840
crossing the floor tells us where
argument,

587
00:35:12.040 --> 00:35:15.660
pressure and refusal are justified, not
who gets to reach for the boot.

588
00:35:16.180 --> 00:35:19.420
The power to judge and the power to impose
are different powers,

589
00:35:19.960 --> 00:35:23.500
and Part 6's conditions bind the second,
not the first.

590
00:35:25.560 --> 00:35:30.040
Before the floor is put to work, the most
serious refusal of it should be heard,

591
00:35:30.140 --> 00:35:31.780
because it comes from the one thinker

592
00:35:31.780 --> 00:35:34.360
who accepted this book's premise most
completely.

593
00:35:35.200 --> 00:35:38.580
Friedrich Nietzsche saw the plurality
before almost anyone:

594
00:35:38.700 --> 00:35:42.700
every people, he wrote, hangs its own
table of goods over itself,

595
00:35:42.740 --> 00:35:45.680
each table the voice of that people's will
to power,

596
00:35:45.740 --> 00:35:48.300
and the tables are created, not found.

597
00:35:48.520 --> 00:35:50.740
So far, this book's own thesis.

598
00:35:51.100 --> 00:35:54.860
But he then did what neither the
relativist nor the floor-builder does:

599
00:35:54.980 --> 00:35:59.660
he ranked the tables -- as symptoms, by
the kind of life, ascending or declining,

600
00:35:59.660 --> 00:36:03.420
that produces each -- while expressly
refusing any floor,

601
00:36:03.420 --> 00:36:05.860
which he diagnosed as nothing neutral at
all,

602
00:36:05.860 --> 00:36:08.960
merely the herd's own thick morality
universalising itself.

603
00:36:11.320 --> 00:36:14.000
That is the exact mirror of the warning
this book has leaned

604
00:36:14.000 --> 00:36:18.020
on since Part 5 about WEIRD values dressed
as universals,

605
00:36:18.020 --> 00:36:19.660
turned against the floor as such,

606
00:36:19.800 --> 00:36:22.440
and I will not pretend to dissolve it in a
paragraph.

607
00:36:24.200 --> 00:36:28.240
What can be stated is its price, and the
scholarship states it too:

608
00:36:28.340 --> 00:36:31.940
his standard smuggles in a substantive
aristocratic ideal

609
00:36:32.200 --> 00:36:35.160
while claiming to stand outside every
morality;

610
00:36:35.300 --> 00:36:38.100
it requires rank between persons and,

611
00:36:38.100 --> 00:36:40.960
he did not flinch from adding, slavery in
some form;

612
00:36:41.460 --> 00:36:45.000
and by its own symptomatology it is itself
a symptom,

613
00:36:45.200 --> 00:36:47.960
one more table hung by one more kind of
life.

614
00:36:48.560 --> 00:36:51.640
An option with a stated price is not a
refutation.

615
00:36:52.080 --> 00:36:56.500
It is the standing reminder that the floor
is a wager to be argued for,

616
00:36:56.580 --> 00:36:58.600
never a discovery to be assumed --

617
00:36:58.620 --> 00:37:01.420
which is exactly how this book has tried
to hold it.

618
00:37:03.220 --> 00:37:06.120
A floor no one has stood on is only a
diagram,

619
00:37:06.160 --> 00:37:10.400
so let me run the test through the hardest
case I own: the book's own hero.

620
00:37:10.760 --> 00:37:14.400
The wong sikep of Part 3 hold, and hold
magnificently:

621
00:37:14.680 --> 00:37:19.020
a complete architecture for living that
carried a community through an empire,

622
00:37:19.020 --> 00:37:22.000
a dictatorship, and a cement company
without breaking.

623
00:37:22.600 --> 00:37:25.520
But the not-crushing side of the test has
to be applied

624
00:37:25.520 --> 00:37:27.700
with the same rigour I asked of everyone
else,

625
00:37:27.880 --> 00:37:31.320
and it finds two places where the holding
pressed hard on the held.

626
00:37:31.780 --> 00:37:32.880
The first is the children.

627
00:37:33.980 --> 00:37:38.180
For most of a century the community
refused to send its children to school,

628
00:37:38.180 --> 00:37:40.740
because the only school on offer was the
state's,

629
00:37:40.960 --> 00:37:45.520
the same state that had fenced the forest
and demanded the taxes; and so,

630
00:37:45.580 --> 00:37:49.900
in a dharma founded by an illiterate
teacher, the children were kept from letters.

631
00:37:50.480 --> 00:37:54.260
Practical reason and education sit
squarely on Nussbaum's list,

632
00:37:54.260 --> 00:37:57.800
and a way of life that denies its children
the tools to examine it,

633
00:37:57.940 --> 00:38:02.060
or to leave it, presses on the floor,
whatever the nobility of the reason.

634
00:38:03.830 --> 00:38:06.720
I celebrated this dharma for a whole part
of this book.

635
00:38:07.160 --> 00:38:10.540
The floor obliges me to say where the
celebration must pause.

636
00:38:11.180 --> 00:38:12.620
The second is the women.

637
00:38:13.280 --> 00:38:14.580
The initiation formula

638
00:38:14.580 --> 00:38:19.440
that Dangir recited in 1928 defines a wife
by her duty to her husband;

639
00:38:19.520 --> 00:38:22.720
the cosmology assimilates woman to the
earth that is worked;

640
00:38:22.920 --> 00:38:26.200
and fieldwork in one Blora village reports
a domestic order in

641
00:38:26.200 --> 00:38:28.620
which Sikep women remain plainly
subordinate.

642
00:38:29.040 --> 00:38:33.460
And yet it was nine women of the Kendeng
farming communities, Sikep among them,

643
00:38:33.460 --> 00:38:35.900
who put their feet in cement at the palace
gate,

644
00:38:35.900 --> 00:38:38.260
on ground the Sikep dharma had prepared.

645
00:38:39.500 --> 00:38:43.060
Both facts are true at once, and they must
be held together:

646
00:38:43.060 --> 00:38:46.860
the tradition made the heroine possible,
and prescribed the wife.

647
00:38:48.820 --> 00:38:51.700
So does the keystone case pass its own
book's test?

648
00:38:52.320 --> 00:38:55.640
Not cleanly, and that is exactly what the
floor is for.

649
00:38:56.160 --> 00:38:58.620
It hands down no verdict of good or evil;

650
00:38:58.780 --> 00:39:01.760
it locates the places where the arguing
has to happen,

651
00:39:01.800 --> 00:39:05.260
and here it found them precisely: the
children and the women.

652
00:39:05.980 --> 00:39:09.280
After independence, the refusal of
schooling softened;

653
00:39:09.400 --> 00:39:11.120
the education studies report that

654
00:39:11.120 --> 00:39:14.460
from this century most Sikep children have
gone to school;

655
00:39:14.460 --> 00:39:17.860
and some communities, still declining the
state's classroom,

656
00:39:17.860 --> 00:39:22.100
have built their own curriculum instead,
teaching their ecology on their own terms.

657
00:39:22.820 --> 00:39:27.480
The hardest edge was adjusted, from
inside, without the dharma losing itself.

658
00:39:28.060 --> 00:39:29.340
That is what it looks like

659
00:39:29.380 --> 00:39:32.720
when the people a dharma binds keep the
power to revise it,

660
00:39:32.900 --> 00:39:35.740
and it is why the floor is a tool for
living traditions,

661
00:39:35.780 --> 00:39:37.500
not a tribunal for dead ones.

662
00:39:39.420 --> 00:39:42.320
The whole standard extends the phrase we
began with.

663
00:39:42.880 --> 00:39:46.420
A dharma is to be judged by how well it
holds without crushing.

664
00:39:47.000 --> 00:39:49.300
The holding is the floor's positive side:

665
00:39:49.340 --> 00:39:52.080
does it actually gather a human life and a
community,

666
00:39:52.080 --> 00:39:54.800
give them shape, meaning and the strength
to endure?

667
00:39:55.500 --> 00:39:57.980
The not-crushing is its protective side:

668
00:39:58.040 --> 00:40:02.380
does it gather without breaking the people
inside it or the strangers outside it?

669
00:40:02.940 --> 00:40:07.580
A dharma that holds but crushes is the
tyranny-and-purity spiral of Part 6;

670
00:40:08.060 --> 00:40:12.800
one that refuses to crush but cannot hold
is the thin gruel of Part 7,

671
00:40:12.860 --> 00:40:15.100
the patchwork that never quite binds.

672
00:40:15.540 --> 00:40:17.080
The good ones do both.

673
00:40:17.540 --> 00:40:20.360
They hold firmly enough to give a life its
spine,

674
00:40:20.380 --> 00:40:24.880
and loosely enough to leave room for other
lives, and other dharmas, to do the same.

675
00:40:25.400 --> 00:40:28.120
That is the answer to whether some are
better: yes.

676
00:40:28.680 --> 00:40:33.280
The better ones hold without crushing,
remain revisable by the people they bind,

677
00:40:33.280 --> 00:40:35.320
and leave the door open for their
neighbours.

678
00:40:37.140 --> 00:40:38.740
Berlin would add a caution:

679
00:40:38.780 --> 00:40:42.560
the standard identifies failures but does
not rank successes,

680
00:40:42.620 --> 00:40:45.700
because above the floor the goods
genuinely conflict.

681
00:40:46.500 --> 00:40:50.100
Everything up there is still to argue
about, and WB Gallie,

682
00:40:50.100 --> 00:40:53.060
whom we met in Part 1, explained why it
never stops:

683
00:40:53.520 --> 00:40:58.860
some concepts (justice, democracy, and
surely dharma) are essentially contested,

684
00:40:59.080 --> 00:41:02.020
disputed from the inside as part of how
they work.

685
00:41:02.580 --> 00:41:05.700
That endless argument is not the failure
of the project.

686
00:41:06.500 --> 00:41:07.720
It is the project.

687
00:41:10.960 --> 00:41:11.980
That which holds

688
00:41:14.260 --> 00:41:16.040
We have come the whole way round.

689
00:41:16.780 --> 00:41:20.500
We started with a word we thought meant
incense and inner peace,

690
00:41:20.600 --> 00:41:23.600
and we followed it down to a plain and
sturdy root:

691
00:41:23.720 --> 00:41:27.800
dharma, that which holds, the beam that
keeps the roof up,

692
00:41:27.880 --> 00:41:31.580
the thing that keeps a life, or a world,
from chaos.

693
00:41:32.400 --> 00:41:34.920
Dharmas are what hold human beings
together.

694
00:41:35.470 --> 00:41:36.720
There are many of them.

695
00:41:37.200 --> 00:41:39.240
They are made, not found.

696
00:41:40.260 --> 00:41:44.200
And the making has now become conscious,
and urgent, and ours.

697
00:41:45.940 --> 00:41:48.600
For all of history we built the things
that hold us

698
00:41:48.600 --> 00:41:51.420
and then knelt before them as if we had
received them.

699
00:41:52.140 --> 00:41:53.380
That age is ending,

700
00:41:53.380 --> 00:41:57.060
not because we grew wiser but because the
concealment wore through.

701
00:41:57.260 --> 00:41:59.240
We can see the workshop now,

702
00:41:59.240 --> 00:42:02.360
and we are being handed the tools at a
demanding moment:

703
00:42:02.360 --> 00:42:06.560
a planet that needs some shared and gentle
holding to come through its crisis,

704
00:42:06.740 --> 00:42:10.280
and a new kind of mind into which we will
pour some ethic or other,

705
00:42:10.280 --> 00:42:12.040
on purpose or by neglect.

706
00:42:12.660 --> 00:42:16.760
The temptation, now of all moments, will
be to reach for the single answer,

707
00:42:16.820 --> 00:42:21.300
the one official dharma, the machine-readable
verdict that ends the messy argument.

708
00:42:21.900 --> 00:42:25.300
That temptation is the one thing the whole
argument stands against.

709
00:42:25.780 --> 00:42:29.480
The plurality is not the problem to be
solved on the way to the answer.

710
00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:31.540
The plurality is the answer,

711
00:42:31.940 --> 00:42:34.460
fenced by a thin floor that forbids only
cruelty

712
00:42:34.720 --> 00:42:37.480
and demands only that the holding not
become a crushing.

713
00:42:39.440 --> 00:42:44.120
None of us gets to opt out of having a
dharma; each of us is running one right now,

714
00:42:44.200 --> 00:42:47.780
chosen or not, and nowhere along the way
have we met a human life

715
00:42:47.780 --> 00:42:49.520
that holds together without one.

716
00:42:49.900 --> 00:42:53.440
The only real choice is whether we run
them asleep or awake.

717
00:42:54.240 --> 00:42:58.400
To practise a dharma awake is to know that
it is made and not found,

718
00:42:58.580 --> 00:43:01.580
to know that it is one good way among
other good ways,

719
00:43:01.720 --> 00:43:04.480
to hold it firmly enough that it actually
holds us,

720
00:43:04.520 --> 00:43:08.380
and lightly enough that it never has to
crush anyone else to do its work.

721
00:43:08.780 --> 00:43:11.640
The woman on the cushion, alone with her
scraps,

722
00:43:11.740 --> 00:43:17.080
has been doing that work all along; what
she lacks is not sincerity but company.

723
00:43:17.600 --> 00:43:20.120
We have always been choosing what holds
us.

724
00:43:20.570 --> 00:43:23.860
Now we get to do it on purpose, with our
eyes open.

725
00:43:24.360 --> 00:43:27.960
That is not a smaller task than the old
religions set themselves.

726
00:43:28.480 --> 00:43:30.920
And it is, at last, openly ours.

